  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1241 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nIn the wake of the exiting aformentioned upper low, a second upper\nlevel low pressure system will form over southern CA Friday, then\nopen into a trough as it ejects northeastward over the Four\nCorners and CO Sunday night. This slow moving system will again\ndraw extremely rich subtropical moisture northward over the\nforecast area with PWATs reaching around 0.80\" along the central \nmountain chain, and as much as 1.20\" farther east. The GEFS \nEnsemble shows PWATs peaking around 160-260% of normal across the \neast Saturday morning. Thus, after a period of less rain in \nbetween systems Friday afternoon, rain coverage and precipitation \nintensity will increase again Friday night through Saturday,\nmainly over central and eastern areas. Rainfall then looks to \npersist over eastern areas through Sunday night. Rain amounts \nFriday night through Sunday night are currently progged to reach \naround 1-3\" across much of the eastern plains with locally higher \namounts. There may also be some snow on the highest peaks of the \ncentral mountain chain Friday night through Sunday night, where \n700 mb temperatures look to bottom out around +2 to +4 C. High \ntemperatures at the surface will continue to trend cooler through \nthe weekend, when readings should vary from a few to around 8 \ndegrees below 30-year averages.\n\nDrier and warmer weather is forecast early in the coming work week\nas a low amplitude ridge of high pressure crosses from the west.",
